    "bodyHtml": "<h4>Why this caught my attention</h4>\n<p>Terence Tao&#x27;s ICM 2026 public lecture and companion essay (<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.16753\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">arXiv:2608.16753</a>) steps past the usual hype about what AI can or cannot prove, and asks a deeper question that resonates strongly in chemistry and materials science as well: what are the real goals and values of scientific research when problem-solving becomes abundant?</p>\n<h4>Key ideas from Tao&#x27;s essay</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The research pipeline goes far beyond solving a problem:</strong> Tao breaks research into five stages: generating a proof, verifying it, clear exposition, community acceptance, and canonicalization into textbooks and shared theory. AI accelerates the first two stages, while the latter three remain fundamentally human.</li>\n<li><strong>The risk of &quot;proof indigestion&quot;:</strong> When automated tools produce valid but opaque outputs faster than humans can audit or digest them, peer review and genuine scientific understanding come under strain.</li>\n<li><strong>Culture, credit, and transparency:</strong> Tao argues for valuing synthesis, refereeing, exposition, human intellectual responsibility, and open disclosure of AI assistance.</li>\n</ul>",
